<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/expert/deep-history-of-our-species">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Deep history is the synthesis field: <strong>genomics, archaeology and anthropology writing
    humanity's story together</strong> — a decade-old capability (ancient DNA at scale) that has
    already rewritten the narrative of migrations, mixture and cultural evolution.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three clocks, the expansions, admixture, coevolution" widget="equationStep" config={deepCfg} />

  <h2>Landmark results of the synthesis</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Indo-European question largely settled (2023→):</strong> genetics + linguistics converge on steppe origin for the family (the linguistics lesson's PIE) — a 200-year debate moved by ancient DNA more than by a century of argument.</li>
    <li><strong>The Americas peopled ~20–15 kya</strong> (later refined by ancient genomes): a movement along the coast, then splits — the genetics of Indigenous American populations preserves the itinerary.</li>
    <li><strong>Population replacements are real:</strong> Britain changed majority ancestry at least twice in 3,000 years (Neolithic → Bell Beaker) without slaughter necessarily — migration and differential reproduction can replace a gene pool quietly.</li>
    <li><strong>The ethical frontier:</strong> ancient DNA research uses Indigenous remains — the field is rewriting consent and collaboration norms with descendant communities (the ethics of studying people who cannot consent, and their relatives who can).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Reich, Who We Are and How We Got Here (2018); Lazaridis et al. (2015); Raghavan et al. on the Americas; Nobel 2022 lecture.</p>
</TopicPage>
